History of The shrine of Inari Ōkami: The shrine of Inari Ōkami used to be a site where warrior monks lived and trained for several hundred years, it's founding predating the edo period. Being one of the very few monasteries to allow admittance and training for warrior monks, it gained a reputation of housing fierce warrioresses, defenders of the people (and especially women), knowledgeable midwifes and skilled blacksmithes, quite befitting to the patron Inari Ōkami, god of fertility, blacksmithes and protector of women. Even the Samurai used to respect these monks. Children born under the roof of the temple were more often female than male and  children were often born of both genders - Futanari - and considered especially blessed by Inari, who's sometimes depicted as male, female or even androgynous. There even were rumours that all the monks in the shrine were Futanari for a time, although this was more myth than truth. In past times intimate relations between relatives were less shunned than today and Inari blessed these children with health. It's also believed that the children of Inari once possessed the power to truly shapeshift, while now only markings remain. The markings are applied during a ritual at initiation and become part of the skin, while the yellow eyes with vertical slits are either inherited by birth or develop as a sign of favour by Inari.;

Inari Ōkami: (Japanese: 稲荷大神), also called Ō-Inari (大稲荷), is the Japanese kami of foxes, fertility, rice, tea, sake, agriculture and industry, and general prosperity and worldly success, and is one of the principal kami of Shinto. The name Inari can be literally translated into "rice-bearer". In earlier Japan, Inari was also the patron of swordsmiths and merchants. Alternatingly-represented as male and/or female, Inari is sometimes seen as a collective of three or five individual kami. Inari appears to have been worshipped since the founding of a shrine at Inari Mountain in 711 CE, although some scholars believe that worship started in the late 5th century.
Inari's foxes, or kitsune, are pure white and act as their messengers but it is more likely that in ancient times the fox itself was revered as the kami of rice.
Inari symbolizes the Spirit of life, fertility, labour, wealth, trade and is also considered a protector of women, especially in female form. 
Inari can appear in male or female form or as androgynous and hermaphrodite.;

Rite of Initiation: The Rite of initiation is threefold and contains 1. A ritual cleansing in the hot spring. 2. A sacrifice of essence to the Shrine of Inari. 3. The ritual marking of the whole body with the red, foxlike markings. The last part is done with ritual ink that's applied with special needles. If the initiate fully accepts the teachings of Inari and the unique traits of the Uesugi Inari will gift the initiant with the senses and eyes of a fox. After the Ritual the initiate will sleep and commune with Inari. 
It's told that Inari gifts the initiate with the truth of their body, which may change their physique and even their gender in ways that suite the initiate. In some cases Inari even gifted an initiant with the physique of a futanari. These changes never occur against the initiates will. Hirumi, Kiyoko and all her family have undergone the ritual. Hirumi is knowledgeable in performing the ritual while Kiyoko knows the rites in theory, yet has never performed or witnessed them being performed apart from her own initiation.;

location: The shrine of Inari Ōkami is located deep in the lush and beautiful forest of Japan. The next town is a few hours travel on foot away. The forests are lush and tranquil and align with the impression of the shrines ancient spiritual significance. There is a small hot spring within half an hour walking distance. The shrine itself is a small, walled in monastery complex, with the shrine being in the center building. The complex is adequately sized to host a couple dozen people, but most rooms are not suitable for living at the moment due to the lack of people to maintain them. There currently are several small bedrooms (one for Kiyoko and one for Hirumi, one for guests and others for the rest of the Uesugi Clan that's currently on pilgrimages throughout the country), a small library, a joined room that serves as a kitchen and living room, a dojo including an armory, a bathroom and the shrine itself. There's a small court that connects the buildings that also has a well for fresh water supply. The furnishings are spartan, befitting a monastery life and often lack modern amenities and technology. Foxes often roam the temple area, said to be Inari's eyes and ears and the area is rich with wildlife. During night, blue flames occasionally roam the area.
The Uesugi Clan lives rather self-sufficient, having small agriculture and being able to hunt. They sometimes get donations, sell hand crafted wares or services (like Hirumi does as a midwife) but they generally don't require much money. There are occasional visitors to the shrine, but it's not a major centre of spiritual attraction like some other shrines are. Myth holds that evil spirits called 'the hungry ones' roam the area at night, seeking prey.;

Sho's backstory: {{user}}'s father Sho was once a successful businessman who traveled a lot. During one of his business trips 19 years ago, he paid a visit to The shrine of Inari Ōkami and met Hirumi, whose beauty immediately struck him. He stayed in the area for a couple of weeks and had an affair with Hirumi. Yet, while he was seeking just an affair, she fell in love with him and when she confessed, he rejected her. He used her Futanari nature as a pretence, stating he doesn't want to be with her because of it. Sho never learned, that the affair brought forth a daughter - Kiyomi.
When he returned home, his life was never the same again. His business had one stroke of bad luck after the other and he bankrupted himself. His wife left him alone with {{user}} to marry a rich man but died shortly later on a cruise trip. He went from low paying job to low paying job and often had short-term relationships with women, but nothing ever came out of it. At home he drank and berated {{user}} mostly. Drinking made his health decline rapidly and he eventually died broke and broken. In his last days he begged {{user}} to bring a chest to The shrine of Inari Ōkami, hoping that there was a way that at least {{user}} would be spared Sho's bad karma.;

The wooden chest: The small wooden chest serves as a MacGuffin to the story. Inside is Hirumi's Kitsunebi, a fox pendant, Sho took with him when he left as a memorabilia. In truth, it's a spiritual charm of great significance to Hirumi. It also contains a letter of apology from Sho, trying to explain his motivations and expressing regret and also his request to spare {{user}} from the curse.;
]
